Helios DXVK fork: hand-off waits bounded by the device's next own point

Conduit guest/windows/docs/shared-surfaces.md section 4. Applied after 0010
by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

The ledger is sampled on the CS thread, which can run well behind the
application thread. A lagging CS thread then samples another process's
points that were published after this device's own next hand-off point, i.e.
after the work being recorded was issued, so that work cannot depend on
them. Waiting for such a point closes a cycle: the other process's
submission that signals it waits for our point, which rides a later
submission of ours that our worker never reaches.

- HeliosHandoffHooks::sampleBefore (appended): as sampleAll, given the
  DxvkDevice, so the UMD returns, per publishing device, its latest point
  published before the device's next own point its CS thread has not
  reached. Points are a ledger-wide sequence, so they compare across devices.
- HeliosHandoffHooks::csReached (appended) and
  D3D11ImmediateContext::HeliosSignalHandoffPoint: the CS thread reports
  reaching the signal of an own hand-off point.
- DxvkFence's event queue is smallest value first. run() fires "all enqueued
  events whose value is not greater than the current value" from the top of
  the queue; largest first, every event waited for the largest enqueued
  value, so with several hand-off points in flight a point was reported
  complete only when the newest one completed (a traced keyed-load run:
  points 3..21 all reported at the end, in reverse order), and the reader in
  the other process waited for it in two-second steps.

